===Etymology===
{{unk|en}}. Attested from a 1959 episode of the UK TV series ''{{w|Hancock's Half Hour}}''. The writers ({{w|Galton and Simpson}}) state that the phrase was in general use when the show was broadcast.<ref name="pf">{{R:Phrase Finder|entry=A load of codswallop|url=codswallop|accessdate=26 February 2017}}</ref><ref name="oed">{{cite-web|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20090305064036/http://oed.com/bbcwords/codswallop-new.html|url=http://oed.com/bbcwords/codswallop-new.html|archivedate=2009–03–09|year=2006|month=January|title=codswallop|work=OED Online}}</ref> A national TV appeal in the UK in 2006 failed to find earlier references,<ref name="pf"/> though a humorous newspaper column from 1947 does use the fictional name "Sir Aubrey Codswallop".<ref>{{cite-journal |work=Reveille |page=5 |date=1947-07-22 |title=The Man Himself |author="Dee" |location=London |passage=What sort of a man is Sir Aubrey Codswallop, the big noise behind the new Utility Mousetrap (Control of Cheese) Order?}}</ref>  Originally written (1963) {{m|en||codswallop}}; the spelling {{m|en|cod's wallop}} is later.

Various etymologies are proposed from some sense of {{m|en|cod}}, such as from {{af|en|cod|t1=joke, imitation|-s-|wallop|t3=beer|qq3=slang}}, hence “imitation beer” (with [[interconsonantal]] ''-s-'' to ease pronunciation of ''-dw-''), or from {{af|en|cod|t1=scrotum, as in {{m|en|codpiece}}|-s-|wallop|t3=to hit}}, hence “to hit (with) the testicle bag,” or from {{m|en|cod||fish}}, hence perhaps some part of the fish, as used in the fishing industry.

A frequently given etymology, although widely rejected as a {{glossary|folk etymology}}, derives it from {{w|Hiram Codd}}, British soft drink maker of the 1870s, known for the eponymous {{w|Codd-neck bottle}}, with the suggestion that {{m|en||codswallop}} is a derisive term for soft drinks by beer drinkers, from {{m|en||Codd’s}} + {{m|en|wallop||beer}}, thus sarcastically “Codd’s beer”. There is no evidence that early uses had this sense; the slang term {{m|en|wallop||beer}} appeared after Codd’s lifetime,<ref name="pf"/> initial spellings (in print from 1963) do not reflect such a derivation ({{m|en||*Codd’s wallop}} and {{m|en||*coddswallop}} with ''-dd-'' are not found), and there is an 80-year gap between the proposed coinage and attestation.

This is also the name given to the wooden device placed over the neck of a codd bottle and given a push (wallop) to dislodge the marble in the neck of the bottle. The word has also been used to describe the process of opening a codd bottle.

